            <chunk name="Object" index="29">
              <items count="2">
                <item name="GUID" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">59e0b89a-e487-49f8-bab8-b5bab16be14c</item>
                <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Panel</item>
              </items>
              <chunks count="1">
                <chunk name="Container">
                  <items count="8">
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">A panel for custom notes and text values</item>
                    <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">f7c639b0-59e3-5a01-971b-d89fab9eefce</item>
                    <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Panel</item>
                    <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">README</item>
                    <item name="Optional" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">false</item>
                    <item name="ScrollRatio" type_name="gh_double" type_code="6">0</item>
                    <item name="SourceCount" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                    <item name="UserText" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">TERRAIN FROM LEVEL CURVES

Builds a site terrain from surveyed level curves (contours drawn at their reduced levels) and a site boundary: points on the curves and on the boundary are triangulated into a Delaunay mesh, re-sectioned across the site and lofted into a smooth terrain surface.

HOW TO USE
1. Sample contours and a boundary are stored in the file, so it solves on open.
2. To use your site: right-click 'Level curves' and Set Multiple Curves (contours drawn at their real heights), then right-click 'Site boundary' and Set one Curve (a closed boundary in plan).
3. 'Points per contour' and 'Section count' control density; 'Section degree' and 'Section points' control smoothing.
4. Bake 'Loft' for the terrain surface.

YOU WILL LEARN
- Rebuild: making curves uniform before sampling
- Dispatch + Closed: separating closed and open contours
- Closest Point: giving boundary corners a height from the nearest contour
- Delaunay Mesh: triangulating scattered points
- Mesh | Plane sections lofted into a surface

READ THE CANVAS LEFT TO RIGHT
Green = inputs you change, coloured boxes = numbered steps, blue = results.

SOURCE
Nono Martínez Alonso (nonoesp) - nonoesp/GettingArchitectureDoneKit
Inbox/More/GADHelper_TerrainFromRLs.gh @ 853a38ceeb
Licence: MIT (see LICENCE panel)
Modified for the Almond parametric library: regrouped, relabelled and annotated; the geometric logic is unchanged.</item>
                  </items>
                  <chunks count="2">
                    <chunk name="Attributes">
                      <items count="5">
                        <item name="Bounds" type_name="gh_drawing_rectanglef" type_code="35">
                          <X>119.0</X>
                          <Y>297.0</Y>
                          <W>400</W>
                          <H>919.5</H>
                        </item>
                        <item name="MarginLeft" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                        <item name="MarginRight" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                        <item name="MarginTop" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                        <item name="Pivot" type_name="gh_drawing_pointf" type_code="31">
                          <X>119.0</X>
                          <Y>297.0</Y>
                        </item>
                      </items>
                    </chunk>
